---
import AutomaticPocketLayout from "../../layouts/AutomaticPocketLayout.astro";
import SupportEmail from "../../components/SupportEmail.astro";
---

<AutomaticPocketLayout
  title="Support"
  description="Playing along with Automatic Pocket, setting up Score my timing, how the score works, and fixes for common problems."
>
  <p>
    Questions, bugs or ideas: email <SupportEmail />. If it's about scoring, say which headphones
    you use.
  </p>

  <h2>Playing along</h2>
  <ol class="list-decimal">
    <li>
      <strong>Pick a beat and a tempo.</strong> Tap the beat's name on the home screen to see all 36,
      and tap one to hear a few bars. Drag the tempo, use − and +, or tap Tap in time.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Press Play.</strong> After a count-in the drums play until you press Stop. The speaker
      is fine; headphones are fine too.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Change things while it plays.</strong> Tap the beat's name to switch beats, the line
      under it to change what the drummer does, and − or + for the tempo. The new drums pick up on
      the same beat of the same bar.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Set up the drummer.</strong> The Fills, Drops out and Tempo drift tiles on the home
      screen open The drummer: how often it fills and for how long, whether it switches to a B
      section every 8 or 16 bars, whether it drops out, and whether it holds the tempo.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Make your own beats.</strong> Tap New beat under My beats, or press and hold a built-in
      beat to start from a copy. Tap the grid to place hits; choose Ghost, Normal or Accent first
      for how hard. The beat loops while you draw. Tap Save.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Score my timing</h2>
  <p>
    Score my timing on the home screen is the optional exercise: play a bass note on every kick, and
    the app listens through the microphone and shows how early or late each one was. It needs a
    little setup the drums don't.
  </p>
  <ol class="list-decimal">
    <li>
      <strong>Put headphones on.</strong> Scoring needs them: on the device's speaker, the drums
      cover up your notes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Calibrate your headphones.</strong> Tap the headphones button at the top of the home
      screen to open Audio setup. Don't wear the headphones for this: hold an earbud (or press a
      headphone cup) right against the bottom of the phone, where the mic is, at medium volume. Tap
      Calibrate and keep still while it plays clicks. Each pair of headphones keeps its own
      calibration, so do this once for each.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Check the mic.</strong> In Audio setup, tap Start mic check and play a few notes. Set
      Sensitivity so every note you play counts and nothing else does.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Place the phone.</strong> Put it between you and your amp, about an arm's length from
      the speaker. Playing unplugged? Put it close to the strings. A USB audio interface, if you
      have one, is used automatically.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Start a take.</strong> Tap Score my timing, pick a practice program or set the feel and
      length yourself, and tap Start. A scored take plays the beat as written: no fills and no
      switch-ups.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>How the score works</h2>
  <p>
    For each kick, the app takes your closest note within 75 ms. Notes that aren't near a kick don't
    count for or against you.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sitting a little ahead of or behind the kick is free. Most players do it, and up to your
    strictness setting (25 ms on Relaxed, 15 on Normal, 8 on Tight) it costs nothing. The feel
    target moves where that spot is: laid back aims behind the kick, push ahead of it.
  </p>
  <p>
    What's scored is how consistent you are around your own spot, times the share of kicks you
    played. On Relaxed, kick notes that vary by about ±10 ms score in the 90s, ±20 ms around 78,
    and ±30 ms around 62.
  </p>

  <h2>Common problems</h2>

  <h3>I can't hear the drums</h3>
  <p>
    Check the volume, and whether the sound is going to headphones or a Bluetooth speaker you
    forgot was connected.
  </p>

  <h3>"Scoring needs headphones"</h3>
  <p>
    The app won't start a scored take on the speaker, because the drums would drown out your bass
    in the mic. Connect wired, USB-C or Bluetooth headphones. Press Play on the home screen to play
    along on the speaker, with no scoring.
  </p>

  <h3>It misses notes I play</h3>
  <p>
    Raise Sensitivity in Audio setup and use the mic check to confirm every note counts. Make sure
    the app can use the mic: Settings › Automatic Pocket › Microphone. Moving the phone closer to
    the amp or the strings helps too.
  </p>

  <h3>It counts notes I didn't play</h3>
  <p>
    Usually drums leaking from the headphones into the mic, or string noise. Closed-back headphones
    or earbuds help, as does turning the drums down a little or lowering Sensitivity.
  </p>

  <h3>Every note looks early, or every note looks late</h3>
  <p>
    Calibrate the headphones you're using now. If you know you're on the kick and the app still
    disagrees, use Fine-tune in Audio setup: slide right if it says you're behind.
  </p>

  <h3>A warning that your Bluetooth device keeps adding delay</h3>
  <p>
    Some Bluetooth devices add more delay the longer they play. One we tested drifted by about
    65 ms over its first minute. The app can't correct a delay that keeps moving, so the later in a
    take, the later your notes look. Use wired or USB-C headphones, or Bluetooth headphones whose
    delay holds steady (AirPods did when we tested them). Calibration tells you which kind you
    have. Playing along without a score isn't affected.
  </p>

  <h3>The score seems low</h3>
  <p>
    The results screen shows where it went: kicks you missed, how far your notes spread around
    your spot, and the notes a listener would actually hear as off. Relaxed is the most forgiving
    strictness.
  </p>

